<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timurid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Timurid, Timurids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Timurid Empire was the state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in the late fourteenth century, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. After Timur's death it fragmented among his descendants but became famous for its patronage of Persian art, architecture, astronomy and literature. It was eventually overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, and the Timurid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timurid-empire.jpg" alt="Timurid Empire" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DigitalNomadWikiKzRu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timurid_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…November 1467 he surprised and killed the Qara Qoyunlu ruler Jahan Shah in eastern Anatolia, and in 1469 he trapped the <b>Timurid</b> sultan Abu Sa’id, who had invaded Azerbaijan, by cutting his supplies during the winter in the Qarabagh plain; Abu Sa’id was captured and put to death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Chagataid domains produced Timur (Tamerlane, 1336–1405), a member of the Turkicised Barlas tribe, who built the <b>Timurid Empire</b>, the most powerful state of the late fourteenth- and fifteenth-century Islamic world, whose capital at Samarkand became under Timur and his successors — notably the astronomer-prince Ulugh Beg…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sayyid dynasty that followed (1414–1451) ruled as nominal vassals of the <b>Timurids</b> over a much-reduced territory around Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Babur, a <b>Timurid</b> prince from the Fergana Valley descended from Timur on his father’s side and from Genghis Khan on his mother’s, capitalised on the political fragmentation of North India and the weakness of the Delhi Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Then came Timur: in 1400 the <b>Timurids</b> defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and Qara Yusuf fled to the Mamluk Sultanate, where Sultan an-Nasir Faraj, unwilling to anger Timur, imprisoned him with the Jalayirid sultan Ahmad Jalayir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibrahim I submitted to Timur in 1386 and, with <b>Timurid</b> support, held off the Golden Horde, but in 1412 he and his ally Constantine I of Georgia were defeated by Qara Yusuf of the Qara Qoyunlu, and Ibrahim was captured and later ransomed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
